-- r 3(3): in proceedings under a judgment or order in an action for administration of an estate, where a person who is not a party
-- makes a claim for a debt or liability, no party other than the executors or administrators is entitled to appear "without the
-- permission of the Court".  The bar is lifted by the permission (QA): the question "is the party entitled to appear" is then NOTHING.
-- src:4561 | (3) Where, in proceedings under a judgment or order given or made in an action for the administration under the direction of the Court of the estate of a deceased person, a claim in respect of a debt or other liability is made against the estate by a person not a party to the action, no party other than the executors or administrators of the estate is entitled to appear in any proceedings relating to that claim without the permission of the Court, and the Court may direct or allow any other party to appear either in addition to, or in substitution for, the executors or administrators on such terms as to costs or otherwise as the Court thinks fit.
@ref O 32 r 3(3) (src:4561)
GIVEN `the claim is made in proceedings under a judgment or order given in an action for the administration of the estate of a deceased person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claim is in respect of a debt or other liability and is made against the estate by a person not a party to the action` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the party is a party other than the executors or administrators of the estate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 32 r 3(3) a party other than the executors or administrators is entitled to appear` MEANS
    IF `the claim is made in proceedings under a judgment or order given in an action for the administration of the estate of a deceased person` AND `the claim is in respect of a debt or other liability and is made against the estate by a person not a party to the action` AND `the party is a party other than the executors or administrators of the estate`
    THEN IF `the Court has given permission`
         THEN JUST TRUE -- "without the permission of the Court": with the permission of the Court the party is entitled to appear, which is how the sentence reads (open convention question QA)
         ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "no party other than the executors or administrators of the estate is entitled to appear in any proceedings relating to that claim without the permission of the Court"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (3) speaks only of these proceedings and these parties

-- src:4561 | (3) Where, in proceedings under a judgment or order given or made in an action for the administration under the direction of the Court of the estate of a deceased person, a claim in respect of a debt or other liability is made against the estate by a person not a party to the action, no party other than the executors or administrators of the estate is entitled to appear in any proceedings relating to that claim without the permission of the Court, and the Court may direct or allow any other party to appear either in addition to, or in substitution for, the executors or administrators on such terms as to costs or otherwise as the Court thinks fit.
@ref O 32 r 3(3) Court may (src:4561)
