-- r 5(1): "need not be given or made unless in the opinion of the Court the questions at issue ... cannot properly be determined
-- otherwise".  Not satisfied: the text says no (JUST FALSE).  Satisfied: the "unless" lifts the bar and says nothing more (QA): NOTHING.
-- src:4565 | 5. —(1) A judgment or order for the administration or execution under the direction of the Court of an estate or trust need not be given or made unless in the opinion of the Court the questions at issue between the parties cannot properly be determined otherwise than under such a judgment or order. (2) Where an administration action is brought by a creditor of the estate of a deceased person or by a person claiming to be entitled under a will or on the intestacy of a deceased person or to be beneficially entitled under a trust, and the claimant alleges that no or insufficient accounts have been provided by the personal representatives (as the case may be) then, without affecting its other powers, the Court may — ( a )
@ref O 32 r 5(1) (src:4565)
GIVEN `in the opinion of the Court the questions at issue between the parties cannot properly be determined otherwise than under such a judgment or order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 32 r 5(1) a judgment or order for administration or execution need be given or made` MEANS
    IF `in the opinion of the Court the questions at issue between the parties cannot properly be determined otherwise than under such a judgment or order`
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the "unless" lifts the "need not" and the paragraph does not say that such a judgment or order must then be given (open convention question QA)
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "need not be given or made unless in the opinion of the Court the questions at issue between the parties cannot properly be determined otherwise than under such a judgment or order"

-- src:4565 | 5. —(1) A judgment or order for the administration or execution under the direction of the Court of an estate or trust need not be given or made unless in the opinion of the Court the questions at issue between the parties cannot properly be determined otherwise than under such a judgment or order. (2) Where an administration action is brought by a creditor of the estate of a deceased person or by a person claiming to be entitled under a will or on the intestacy of a deceased person or to be beneficially entitled under a trust, and the claimant alleges that no or insufficient accounts have been provided by the personal representatives (as the case may be) then, without affecting its other powers, the Court may — ( a )
